Gastric dilatation-volvulus — bloat — is one of the most time-sensitive emergencies in veterinary medicine. Large, deep-chested dogs are at the highest risk, but it can happen to any breed. Hours matter.

## What you'll see

The classic cluster:

- **A visibly distended, drum-tight abdomen**
- **Unproductive retching** (looks like trying to throw up but nothing comes out)
- **Restless pacing**, unable to lie down comfortably
- **Drooling, panting, anxious behavior**
- **Pale or grey gums**, weakness as it progresses

Any one of these on its own is worth a phone call. Two or more together is an emergency. Do not wait until morning. Do not give Pepto. Get in the car.

## Why minutes matter

In GDV, the stomach twists and cuts off its own blood supply. Tissue dies. Shock follows. Without surgical correction, mortality is high — but **survival rates climb dramatically when treatment begins within the first few hours**.

## What hospital staff will likely do

A quick X-ray confirms the diagnosis. They will stabilize blood pressure, decompress the stomach, and prep for emergency surgery. Estimates run several thousand dollars.

## If cost is the barrier

This is exactly the kind of case The Wolf Project exists to fund. Treatable. Time-sensitive. Cost as the only obstacle. If you're in that situation right now and your hospital has a partner referral, ask them to mention us.

